7. Recommended hierarchy

The most important part is to establish a consistent hierarchy:

DOMAIN
  │
  └── CAPABILITY
        │
        └── BUSINESS PROCESS
              │
              └── JOURNEY
                    │
                    ├── SCENARIO
                    │
                    └── STEPS
                          │
                          ├── KEYWORD
                          ├── SYSTEM
                          ├── SERVICE
                          ├── COMPONENT
                          ├── EXPECTED OUTCOME
                          └── ACTUAL OUTCOME

For your Java and .NET Core implementation, I would make this hierarchy language-neutral and have both Cucumber implementations produce the same Journey Event/Metadata model. That gives you one reporting platform even when the underlying automation is split between Java and .NET.

1. Define a standard Journey model with Journey ID, name, domain, capability, business process, criticality, actor, entry/exit points, and expected outcome.
2. Establish a controlled Keyword library for actions such as CREATE, SUBMIT, VALIDATE, AUTHORIZE, RELEASE, PUBLISH, VERIFY, RECONCILE, RETRY, and WAIT.
3. Define standard Tag categories for journey, business, test, environment, requirement, technical, execution, and traceability metadata.
4. Create a common metadata schema that works consistently across Java Cucumber and .NET Core Cucumber implementations.
5. Add Scenario and Step IDs to uniquely identify every business workflow and individual action.
6. Capture system, service, component, API, database, messaging, and external-system information for each step.
7. Support step dependencies, sequence, parallel groups, optional steps, retries, and asynchronous/wait operations.
8. Capture expected outcome, actual outcome, business outcome, technical outcome, duration, and failure reason.
9. Build a Journey Report showing the complete business flow, step status, system interactions, failures, KPIs, and traceability.
10. Integrate execution ID, correlation ID, trace ID, environment, Kubernetes/OpenShift details, logs, screenshots, and API evidence for end-to-end troubleshooting.
